## DESCRIPTION
##  For a list on a restaurant questionaire, determine if the questions reference a
## variable of type: Nominal, Ordinal or Interval.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## JJH tagged this problem.

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Classifying data)
## Date(5/25/2005)
## Institution(UVA)
## Author(Jeff Holt)
## Level(3)
## TitleText1('Statistics for Management and Economics')
## AuthorText1('Keller, Warrack')
## EditionText1('6')
## KEYWORDS('statistics', 'descriptive statistics', 'interval', 'nominal', 'ordinal')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

@questions = (
    'Have you eaten at this restaurant previously?',
    'Which of the following attributes of this restaurant do you find most '
        . 'attractive: service, prices, quality of food, menu options?',
    'Would your overall rating of this restaurant be excellent, good, '
        . 'fair, or poor?',
    'Do you consider our prices to be high, average, or low?',
    'Would you recommend this restaurant to a friend?',
    'How likely are you to recommend this restaurant to others: not at all '
        . 'likely, somewhat likely, very likely, or definitely?',
    'In which of the following areas does this restaurant most need improvement: '
        . 'service, wait time, quality of food?'
);

@answers = (
    'Nominal', 'Nominal', 'Ordinal', 'Ordinal',
    'Nominal', 'Ordinal', 'Nominal'
);

@slice = random_subset(4, 0 .. $#questions);

@opts = ('Interval', 'Nominal', 'Ordinal');

@dd = map { DropDown([@opts], $answers[$_]) } @slice;

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
BEGIN_PGML
Before leaving a particular restaurant, patrons are asked to respond to a questionaire
containing the questions given below.  For each question, indicate (using the pull-down menu) whether the possible responses are Nominal or Ordinal.
  
1) [$questions[$slice[0]]] [_]{$dd[0]}
2) [$questions[$slice[1]]] [_]{$dd[1]}
3) [$questions[$slice[2]]] [_]{$dd[2]}
4) [$questions[$slice[3]]] [_]{$dd[3]}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Recall that 

- *Ordinal* variables are categorical variables that have values that can be ordered.
- *Nominal* variables are categorical variables that in general are not orderable.
- *Interval* variables are numerical variables in a range. 

The answers:

1) [$questions[$slice[0]]] *[$dd[0]]****
2) [$questions[$slice[1]]] *[$dd[1]]****
3) [$questions[$slice[2]]] *[$dd[2]]****
4) [$questions[$slice[3]]] *[$dd[3]]****
END_PGML_SOLUTION

COMMENT(
    'Questions selected randomly from a larger set.  See source for full question list.'
);
ENDDOCUMENT();        # This should be the last executable line in the problem.